# Product thesis

This is the business:

> Bring a book you have the right to read. We tutor you through your copy. We do not become the library.

Wealth of Nations is the **public demo** so a visitor can try the loop without uploading. It is not the product. The product name is **Ken**.

## We do

- Ingest a copy the signed-in user has the right to read (private to that account).
- Turn that copy into gated close-reading units; tutor from the page; test only when they ask; judge against the author's claims; cite into their reader.
- Keep Smith as a zero-upload try path with the same loop.

## We refuse

- Hosting or sharing a book so others can read it here (no class library, no "upload once for the seminar").
- Replacing the publisher's copy so students do not buy the book.
- Homework-helper mode (give the answer, skip the argument).
- Treating Smith-specific parsers or prompts as the long-term architecture. Generic ingest + "this author's argument" is the product; Smith is a fixture of the demo corpus.

## Implications for code

- Per-user corpora and progress, never a global catalog of uploaded files.
- No share-the-file or share-the-full-text features. Progress reports must not attach the source.
- Public demo corpus (Smith) is the only book everyone sees.
- Tutor prompts must be parameterizable by work, not hard-coded to Adam Smith, except for demo flavor on that corpus.
